[s0l0!-header][h1]Structures with Structures[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The members of a structure may also be structures. 
For example, consider the following statements:[code format="c" codeId="1770"]typedef struct {
  int x;
  int y;
} point;

typedef struct {
  float radius;
  [b]point[/b] center;
} circle; [/code]
Nested curly braces are used to initialize members that are structs. The dot operator is used twice to access members of members, as in the statements:[code format="c" codeId="1771"]circle c = {4.5, {1, 3}};
printf("%3.1f %d,%d", c.radius, [b]c.center.x[/b], [b]c.center.y[/b]);
/* 4.5  1,3 */
 [/code]
[note]A struct definition must appear before it can be used inside another struct.[/note]

