[s0l0!-header][h1]Pointers to Structures[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Just like pointers to variables, pointers to structures can also be defined.

[b]struct myStruct *struct_ptr;[/b]
defines a pointer to the [i]myStruct[/i] structure.

[b]struct_ptr = &struct_var;[/b]
stores the address of the structure variable [i]struct_var[/i] in the pointer [i]struct_ptr[/i].

[b]struct_ptr -> struct_mem;[/b]
accesses the value of the structure member [i]struct_mem[/i].

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1772"]struct student{
  char name[50];
  int number;
  int age;
};

// Struct pointer as a function parameter
void showStudentData([b]struct student *st[/b]) {
  printf("\nStudent:\n");
  printf("Name: %s\n", st[b]->[/b]name);
  printf("Number: %d\n", st[b]->[/b]number);
  printf("Age: %d\n", st[b]->[/b]age);
}

struct student st1 = {"Krishna", 5, 21};
showStudentData([b]&st1[/b]);[/code]
The [b]->[/b] operator allows to access members of the struct though the pointer.
[note][b](*st).age[/b] is the same as [b]st->age[/b].
Also, when a [b]typedef[/b] has been used to name the struct, then a pointer is declared using only the typedef name along with * and the pointer name.[/note]

